</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Hunter, A.W., M. Caplow, D.L. Coy, W.O. Hancock, S. Diez, L. Wordeman, and J. Howard. <year>2003</year>. The kinesin-related protein MCAK is a microtubule depolymerase that forms an ATP-hydrolyzing complex at microtubule ends. <source>Mol. Cell.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>445</fpage>&#x02013;457.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12620232</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Hyman, A.A., and E. Karsenti. <year>1996</year>. Morphogenetic properties of microtubules and mitotic spindle assembly. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>84</volume>:<fpage>401</fpage>&#x02013;410.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8608594</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Inoue, S., and E.D. Salmon. <year>1995</year>. Force generation by microtubule assembly/disassembly in mitosis and related movements. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>6</volume>:<fpage>1619</fpage>&#x02013;1640.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8590794</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Jordan, M.A., D. Thrower, and L. Wilson. <year>1992</year>. Effects of vinblastine, podophyllotoxin and nocodazole on mitotic spindles. Implications for the role of microtubule dynamics in mitosis. <source>J. Cell Sci.</source> <volume>102</volume>:<fpage>401</fpage>&#x02013;416.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1506423</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Kinoshita, K., I. Arnal, A. Desai, D.N. Drechsel, and A.A. Hyman. <year>2001</year>. Reconstitution of physiological microtubule dynamics using purified components. <source>Science.</source> <volume>294</volume>:<fpage>1340</fpage>&#x02013;1342.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11701928</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Kline-Smith, S.L., and C.E. Walczak. <year>2002</year>. The microtubule-destabilizing kinesin XKCM1 regulates microtubule dynamic instability in cells. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>2718</fpage>&#x02013;2731.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12181341</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Kline-Smith, S.L., A. Khodjakov, P. Hergert, and C.E. Walczak. <year>2004</year>. Depletion of centromeric MCAK leads to chromosome congression and segregation defects due to improper kinetochore attachments. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>1146</fpage>&#x02013;1159.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14699064</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Levesque, A.A., and D.A. Compton. <year>2001</year>. The chromokinesin Kid is necessary for chromosome arm orientation and oscillation, but not congression, on mitotic spindles. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>154</volume>:<fpage>1135</fpage>&#x02013;1146.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11564754</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Mack, G.J., and D.A. Compton. <year>2001</year>. Analysis of mitotic microtubule-associated proteins using mass spectrometry identifies astrin, a spindle-associated protein. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>14434</fpage>&#x02013;14439.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11724960</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Maddox, P., A. Straight, P. Coughlin, T.J. Mitchison, and E.D. Salmon. <year>2003</year>. Direct observation of microtubule dynamics at kinetochores in <italic>Xenopus</italic> extract spindles: implications for spindle mechanics. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>162</volume>:<fpage>377</fpage>&#x02013;382.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12900391</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Maney, T., A.W. Hunter, M. Wagenbach, and L. Wordeman. <year>1998</year>. Mitotic centromere-associated kinesin is important for anaphase chromosome segregation. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>142</volume>:<fpage>787</fpage>&#x02013;801.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9700166</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Maney, T., M. Wagenbach, and L. Wordeman. <year>2001</year>. Molecular dissection of the microtubule depolymerizing activity of mitotic centromere-associated kinesin. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>276</volume>:<fpage>34753</fpage>&#x02013;34758.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11466324</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Mayer, T.U., T.M. Kapoor, S.J. Haggarty, R.W. King, S.L. Schreiber, and T.J. Mitchison. <year>1999</year>. Small molecule inhibitor of mitotic spindle bipolarity identified in a phenotype-based screen. <source>Science.</source> <volume>286</volume>:<fpage>971</fpage>&#x02013;974.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10542155</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">McIntosh, J.R., and M.P. Koonce. <year>1989</year>. Mitosis. <source>Science.</source> <volume>246</volume>:<fpage>622</fpage>&#x02013;628.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2683078</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Moores, C.A., M. Yu, J. Guo, C. Beraud, R. Sakowicz, and R.A. Milligan. <year>2002</year>. A mechanism for microtubule depolymerization by KinI kinesins. <source>Mol. Cell.</source> <volume>9</volume>:<fpage>903</fpage>&#x02013;909.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11983180</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Morfini, G., S. Quiroga, A. Rosa, K. Kosik, and A. Caceres. <year>1997</year>. Suppression of KIF2 in PC12 cells alters the distribution of a growth cone nonsynaptic membrane receptor and inhibits neurite extension. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>138</volume>:<fpage>657</fpage>&#x02013;669.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9245793</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Mountain, V., C. Simerly, L. Howard, A. Ando, G. Schatten, and D.A. Compton. <year>1999</year>. The kinesin-related protein, HSET, opposes the activity of Eg5 and cross-links microtubules in the mammalian mitotic spindle. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>147</volume>:<fpage>351</fpage>&#x02013;366.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10525540</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Noda, Y., R. Sato-Yoshitake, S. Kondo, M. Nangaku, and N. Hirokawa. <year>1995</year>. KIF2 is a new microtubule-based anterograde motor that transports membranous organelles distinct from those carried by kinesin heavy chain or KIF3A/B. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>129</volume>:<fpage>157</fpage>&#x02013;167.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7535303</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Ogawa, T., R. Nitta, Y. Okada, and N. Hirokawa. <year>2004</year>. A common mechanism for microtubule destabilizers-M type kinesins stabilize curling of the protofilament using the class-specific neck and loops. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>591</fpage>&#x02013;602.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14980225</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Ohi, R., M.L. Coughlin, W.S. Lane, and T.J. Mitchison. <year>2003</year>. An inner centromere protein that stimulates the microtubule depolymerizing activity of a KinI kinesin. <source>Dev. Cell.</source> <volume>5</volume>:<fpage>309</fpage>&#x02013;321.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12919681</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Rogers, G.C., S.L. Rogers, T.A. Schwimmer, S.C. Ems-McClung, C.E. Walczak, R.D. Vale, J.M. Scholey, and D.J. Sharp. <year>2003</year>. Two mitotic kinesins cooperate to drive sister chromatid separation during anaphase. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>427</volume>:<fpage>364</fpage>&#x02013;370.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14681690</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">Sawin, K.E., K. LeFuellec, M. Philippe, and T.J. Mitchison. <year>1992</year>. Mitotic spindle organization by a plus-end-directed microtubule motor. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>359</volume>:<fpage>540</fpage>&#x02013;543.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1406972</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="journal">Sharp, D.J., G.C. Rogers, and J.M. Scholey. <year>2000</year>. Microtubule motors in mitosis. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>407</volume>:<fpage>41</fpage>&#x02013;47.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10993066</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Tournebize, R., A. Popov, K. Kinoshita, A.J. Ashford, S. Rybina, A. Pozniakovsky, T.U. Mayer, C.E. Walczak, E. Karsenti, and A.A. Hyman. <year>2000</year>. Control of microtubule dynamics by the antagonistic activities of XMAP215 and XKCM1 in <italic>Xenopus</italic> egg extracts. <source>Nat. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>13</fpage>&#x02013;19.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10620801</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib37"><citation citation-type="journal">Vale, R.D., and R.J. Fletterick. <year>1997</year>. The design plan of kinesin motors. <source>Annu. Rev. Cell Dev. Biol.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>745</fpage>&#x02013;777.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9442886</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib38"><citation citation-type="journal">Vasquez, R.J., B. Howell, A.M. Yvon, P. Wadsworth, and L. Cassimeris. <year>1997</year>. Nanomolar concentrations of nocodazole alter microtubule dynamic instability in vivo and in vitro. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>973</fpage>&#x02013;985.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9201709</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><citation citation-type="journal">Walczak, C.E., T.J. Mitchison, and A. Desai. <year>1996</year>. XKCM1: a <italic>Xenopus</italic> kinesin-related protein that regulates microtubule dynamics during mitotic spindle assembly. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>84</volume>:<fpage>37</fpage>&#x02013;47.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8548824</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib40"><citation citation-type="journal">Walczak, C.E., E.C. Gan, A. Desai, T.J. Mitchison, and S.L. Kline-Smith. <year>2002</year>. The microtubule-destabilizing kinesin XKCM1 is required for proper chromosome positioning during spindle assembly. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>12</volume>:<fpage>1885</fpage>&#x02013;1889.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12419191</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib41"><citation citation-type="journal">Wordeman, L., and T.J. Mitchison. <year>1995</year>. Identification and partial characterization of mitotic centromere-associated kinesin, a kinesin-related protein that associates with centromeres during mitosis. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>128</volume>:<fpage>95</fpage>&#x02013;104.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7822426</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib42"><citation citation-type="journal">Yao, X., K.L. Anderson, and D.W. Cleveland. <year>1997</year>. The microtubule-dependent motor centromere-associated protein E (CENP-E) is an integral component of kinetochore corona fibers that link centromeres to spindle microtubules. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>139</volume>:<fpage>435</fpage>&#x02013;447.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9334346</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>